// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row stdlib_drop_uninhabited_enum_byvalue_consumer (tier 3). An
// UNINHABITED enum with an `impl Drop` and a by-value consumer fn is legal Rust —
// upstream tests/ui/drop/drop-uninhabited-enum.rs is `//@ run-pass` and is this
// program token for token. TODAY logosc refuses it, and not with a diagnostic:
//   error: 'func.call' op operand type mismatch: expected operand type '!llvm.ptr',
//          but provided 'i32' for operand number 0
//   mlir_gen: module verification failed
// Same family as fatslice_field_match_binder_invalid_mlir: an invalid module, not a
// judgment.
//
// ⚠ WHAT MAKES THIS A ROW RATHER THAN A CURIOSITY — THE ONE-VARIABLE CONTROL. Write
// `trait Drop { fn drop(self: Self); }` in this file and change the receiver to
// `fn drop(self: Foo)`, and the SAME program compiles rc 0. The only difference is
// WHICH `Drop` the impl binds. So the mechanism a user actually gets is broken here
// and the local by-value trait the corpus used to declare hid it. Found 2026-09-10
// by converting tests/imported/pass/drop/drop-uninhabited-enum-b154 off the local
// trait; that fixture is the ONE file of 126 the conversion could not carry, and it
// is left declaring its own `trait Drop` until this row closes.
//
// BOTH HALVES ARE LOAD-BEARING, measured: delete `fn foo` and it compiles clean;
// give `Foo` a variant (`enum Foo { A(i64) }`) and it compiles clean. Only the pair
// of an uninhabited enum and a by-value consumer reaches the bad lowering.
// Rust: exit 0.
package stdlib_drop_uninhabited_enum_byvalue_consumer;

enum Foo {}

impl Drop for Foo {
    fn drop(self: &mut Self) {}
}

fn foo(x: Foo) {}

fn main() -> i32 { return 0i32; }
